NAs in the inla.stack output are not perplexing. As explained in the "read this first" spde tutorial, the very purpose of inla.stack is to remove the need for the user to do tedious constructions adding NAs where needed to construct the desired A*eta predictors. (Which we've further encapsulated in inlabru.org <http://inlabru.org>, so that the user doesn't even need to call inla.stwck themselves...)

But it is perplexing that inla seems to make a factor out of a numeric. The inla.stack.data() output looks fine.  Is stack.est the stack used in the inla call, or do you join it with the prediction stack first? Make sure you check the one that's actually used in the inla() call.

Finn

